 ## 2 Related work
 
-<!-- p:S2-01 -->Work on retrieval for captioning is of two kinds. Method papers propose a captioner or a prompting pipeline that reads retrieved captions [@Ramos2023SmallCap; @Ramos2023EXTRA; @Ramos2023LMCap; @Ibrahim2025CONCAP]. Analysis papers take existing models and ask when and why in-context examples help (Table 1). This study is an analysis and proposes no captioner.
-
-<!-- p:S2-02 -->**The random control exists.** For trained retrieval-augmented captioners, one study evaluates a variant that reads a random caption from the datastore [@Ramos2023EXTRA], and another gives a trained captioner, at the same k, captions retrieved for a different image [@Li2024RetrievalRobustness]. Studies of in-context selection compare similarity-chosen with random examples, mostly in English and with example images [@Yang2023ICLConfig; @Li2025UnveilingICL]. Multimodal in-context learning is reported to be driven mainly by the text of the examples [@Baldassini2024MICL; @Chen2025TrueMICL]. This is the reason for the `rand` control: if the text of the examples alone produces a gain, `rand` shows it as much as `ret` does.
+<!-- p:S2-02 -->**The random control exists.** For trained retrieval-augmented captioners, one study evaluates a variant that reads a random caption from the datastore [@Ramos2023EXTRA], and another gives a trained captioner, at the same k, captions retrieved for a different image [@Li2024RetrievalRobustness]. Studies of in-context selection compare similarity-chosen with random examples, mostly in English and with example images [@Yang2023ICLConfig; @Li2025UnveilingICL].
 
 <!-- p:S2-03 -->**The English precedent.** OpenFlamingo reports that retrieved image-caption examples beat random ones on COCO, and that on Flickr30K they scored lower at the smaller shot counts it reports [@Awadalla2023OpenFlamingo]. Our COCO result therefore repeats a pattern already known for English, under other conditions (example captions as text only, configurations frozen in another language, a single look). It is not a new English finding.
 
-<!-- p:S2-04 -->**Closest multilingual and Vietnamese work.** A training-free multilingual study for remote sensing retrieves in-context examples, with neither an equal-k random control nor a registered test [@Rebelo2025RSCap]. The news-captioning benchmark MUNIChus compares random with similarity-retrieved examples at equal k in several languages that do not include Vietnamese, and reports point scores [@Chen2026MUNIChus]. Two multilingual retrieval-augmented captioners list Vietnamese among their languages, one of them training-free, and neither has a random control [@Ramos2023LMCap; @Ibrahim2025CONCAP]. Other Vietnamese work trains or adapts captioners [@Vu2025VELVET; @Vu2026ViG; @Nguyen2026FAIR]; this study trains nothing.
-
